---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { about, discrepancies } from '../lib/data';
---

<Base title="About" description="About the Indipetae archive: sources, editorial method, and how to cite.">
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> · About</nav>
  <p class="kicker">About this archive</p>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="notice ai-disclaimer" role="note">
    <strong>A note on provenance.</strong> The English translations in this archive were
    produced with the assistance of multiple AI agents, and this website itself was built
    with AI assistance. The result is offered for exploration, prayer, and personal study —
    <strong>it is not intended for academic use or citation</strong>. Scholars should consult
    the original sources named below.
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-collection">The collection</h2>
  <p>
    The <em>litterae indipetae</em> — "letters seeking the Indies" — are petitions written by
    Jesuits asking their Superior General to send them to the foreign missions. This archive
    presents English translations of 169 such letters addressed to
    <a href="/people/vitelleschi-mutio/">Mutio Vitelleschi</a>, Superior General of the
    Society of Jesus from 1615 to 1645, written between 1616 and 1647.
  </p>
  <p>
    The writers — 48 Jesuits, mostly young scholastics in Italy — plead, argue, weep, and
    reason their way toward one request: to spend and be spent for the salvation of souls
    in the Indies, China, Japan, and other distant missions. The letters are at once
    spiritual autobiography, bureaucratic petition, and baroque rhetoric.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <p>{about.note_on_text}</p>
  <p>
    Source: {about.source}. The original letters, written 1616–1647, are in the public
    domain; the English translation is offered for private study.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site preserves the volume's original numbering (Letters 1–169), its
    <a href="/people/">Index of Authors</a> and <a href="/topics/">Index of Topics</a>,
    and its archival references (ARSI, Fondo Gesuitico). Letter 100 is not translated:
    the database transcription is placeholder text, and the absence is preserved rather
    than filled.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editorial-method">Editorial method</h2>
  <ul class="method">
    <li>
      <strong>Faithful text.</strong> Letter texts reproduce the printed translation without
      modernization or rewriting. Typesetter's hyphenation across line breaks has been
      rejoined; paragraphing follows the printed edition.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Desire is not fact.</strong> A petition to be sent to China is evidence of
      desire, not of travel. Relationship types distinguish <em>written by</em>,
      <em>written in</em>, <em>mentions</em>, <em>desires to travel to</em>, and
      <em>indexed under</em> — they are never collapsed into one vague link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stated vs. inferred.</strong> Information explicitly in a letter is shown as
      such; index entries are labeled as index entries; editorial inference (for example,
      automatically detected destination mentions) is backed by a quoted evidence
      snippet on the graph and in the data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Discrepancies preserved.</strong> Where the letter, the printed indexes, and the
      database disagree — on dates or on a destination — the disagreement is recorded
      openly rather than silently resolved.
      {discrepancies.length > 0 && (
        <> Currently recorded: {discrepancies.map((d) => (
          <span><a href={`/letters/${d.letter}/`}>Letter {d.letter}</a> — {d.note} </span>
        ))}</>
      )}
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Missing information.</strong> Where the source gives no place, date, or
      reference, the site says "Not specified in the source" rather than guessing.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>

<style>
  .method li { margin-bottom: .7rem; max-width: 46rem; }
  code { font-size: .9em; background: var(--paper-deep); padding: .1em .35em; border-radius: 4px; }
  .ai-disclaimer {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft);
    padding: .9rem 1.2rem; border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    max-width: 46rem; margin: 1.2rem 0 1.6rem;
  }
</style>
